.\" zxplore.1 — manual page for zxplore, the universal ZFS console.
.\" Format: mdoc(7). Render check: mandoc -Tlint docs/zxplore.1
.Dd July 31, 2026
.Dt ZXPLORE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zxplore
.Nd universal ZFS console for Linux and FreeBSD
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -tui
.Op Fl -version
.Nm
.Fl -containers
.Op Ar command Op Ar name
.Nm
.Fl -builder
.Ar command
.Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Fl -observe
.Op Ar pool
.Op Ar command Op Ar arg
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a direct console for OpenZFS: browse datasets and snapshots with a full
property and permission dossier, design and create pools from the disks a
host has, take and manage snapshots, restore individual files from any
snapshot, replicate datasets between machines, and manage pools, boot
environments, native encryption and the container estate.
.Pp
Every action maps to a plain
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zpool 8 ,
or
.Xr ssh 1
command.
Nothing is hidden and nothing is invented: destructive operations show the
literal command before it runs, and every executed mutation is appended to
an audit log (see
.Sx FILES ) .
.Pp
Two surfaces share one engine:
.Bl -tag -width "zxplore --tui"
.It Nm
the native GUI (keyboard and mouse, right-click menus).
In the static terminal-only build this starts the TUI instead.
.It Nm Fl -tui
the terminal UI, for headless hosts and SSH sessions.
.El
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -tui
Start the terminal UI.
When not running as root,
.Nm
re-executes itself under
.Xr sudo 8
(ZFS administration requires privilege; the terminal keeps the tty).
.It Fl -version , Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl -builder Ar command Op Ar arg ...
The pool Builder from a terminal: the disk shelf, candidate layouts, an
imported pool's vdev tree, a dry run, a create.
Re-executes under
.Xr sudo 8
like
.Fl -tui .
Commands and the vdev spec grammar are in
.Sx THE BUILDER .
.It Fl -containers Op Ar command Op Ar name
The container estate from a terminal: list, snapshot, snapshots, rollback,
replicate.
Commands are in
.Sx CONTAINERS .
.It Fl -observe Op Ar pool Op Ar command Op Ar arg
One second of a pool and the verdicts, from a terminal; the first imported
pool when none is named.
Not elevated: the kstats are world-readable.
Commands are in
.Sx OBSERVE .
.El
.Pp
Every subcommand prints a usage line and exits 2 when called with the wrong
arguments, and prints
.Dq zxplore: Ar reason
to standard error and exits 1 when the host refuses \(em see
.Sx DIAGNOSTICS .
.Sh THE BROWSER
The main view lists every dataset and volume with a pinned pool overview
(health, capacity, fragmentation, scrub state, errors).
The header names the detected stack \(em e.g.
.Dq OpenZFS 2.4.3 on Fedora Linux 44
\(em degrading gracefully where
.Ql zfs version
does not exist (native illumos ZFS, OpenZFS before 0.8).
Selecting a dataset renders its dossier: health verdict, every property
grouped by concern with its source
.Pq local, inherited, received, default ,
POSIX mode and ACLs on the mountpoint, and
.Xr zfs-allow 8
delegations.
Right-clicking a dataset opens the lifecycle menu: create child/volume,
rename, mount, encryption (load/unload/change key, create encrypted),
snapshot, snapshot explorer, diff, clone, replicate, boot environment,
rollback, destroy.
.Pp
On a host with no imported pools \(em or no ZFS at all \(em the browser
shows guidance instead of an empty list: how to install OpenZFS, how to
import or create a pool, or how to connect to a remote ZFS box.
The pool manager can scan for exported pools
.Pq Ql zpool import
and import one, and drill into any pool: vitals and tuning properties, the
vdev topology with per-device error counters, one-shot I/O statistics, the
pool's file layout, and BOTH space accountings \(em what
.Ql zfs list
says (snapshots, children, reservations included) next to what
.Xr df 1
reports, which famously disagree by design.
.Sh SNAPSHOT EXPLORER
The explorer fuses the file level with the snapshot level.
It browses the files of a dataset \(em the live tree, or transparently
inside any snapshot via the
.Pa .zfs/snapshot
directory \(em and for any selected path shows every snapshot that contains
it, with each copy's size and modification time, flagged when a copy
differs from the one in view.
.Pp
Any version can be restored into the live dataset, either overwriting in
place or alongside as
.Pa name.from-SNAPSHOT .
A directory restored over live is MERGED
.Pq files created after the snapshot survive ;
use rollback for a true reset.
The exact
.Xr cp 1
command is shown before anything runs.
.Pp
The diff pane renders
.Ql zfs diff -H
between two snapshots, or between a snapshot and the live filesystem,
colored by change type
.Pq modified, added, removed, renamed
and filterable by path substring.
.Sh THE BUILDER
The Builder tab (F2) designs a pool from the disks the host has and shows
what the design yields before anything is written.
The shelf lists every whole disk with its kind, size and model; a disk that
is in use says why
.Pq Dq mounted at /boot/efi , Dq zfs member of rpool
and cannot be ticked.
Ticking disks for data produces the candidate layouts \(em mirrors, RAIDZ1,
RAIDZ2 and RAIDZ3 split into sane vdev widths, dRAID2 where the disk count
divides, and a stripe \(em each with its usable and raw capacity, what it
survives, a badge and the reason.
Free NVMe or SSD devices next to rotational data are proposed as a mirrored
log device and a cache.
.Pp
The layout shows one row per vdev with the disks as chips; a vdev can be
dropped, and a custom one added with any kind and role (dRAID data disks
per group and spares by hand).
The summary carries the exact
.Xr zpool-create 8
line the design becomes \(em built on
.Pa /dev/disk/by-id
names with
.Cm ashift
and
.Cm compression
set explicitly \(em and the warnings an operator would raise: a stripe of
data, a single log device, a rotational cache, RAIDZ1 across large disks,
a USB bridge.
.Dq Dry run
shows the output of
.Ql zpool create -n ;
.Dq Create
asks for the pool name to be typed and then builds it.
.Dq See a pool
draws an imported pool's vdev tree from
.Ql zpool status -P
in the same rows, state-coloured.
.Pp
Capacity figures are the textbook numbers: parity subtracted, the smallest
member setting the size.
RAIDZ additionally loses a few percent to padding, so the numbers are shown
with \(ap and the dry run is the truth.
The shelf comes from
.Xr lsblk 8 ;
on
.Fx
the tab reports that rather than showing an empty shelf.
.Pp
The same engine from a terminal:
.Bl -tag -width "--builder dry-run NAME SPEC"
.It Nm Fl -builder Cm disks
The shelf: one line per disk with kind, size, model and whether it is free.
Pseudo devices (zvols, loop devices) are hidden unless
.Ev ZXPLORE_BUILDER_ALL_DEVICES
is set to 1, which is how a lab builds on loop files.
.It Nm Fl -builder Cm suggest Ar name Ar disk ...
The candidate layouts for those disks, each with its capacity, what it
survives, the reason, the create line and any warnings.
.It Nm Fl -builder Cm topology Ar pool
An imported pool's vdev tree, indented, with state and error counters.
.It Nm Fl -builder Cm dry-run Ar name Ar spec ...
What zpool would build, verbatim from
.Ql zpool create -n .
.It Nm Fl -builder Cm create Ar name Ar spec ...
Build it.
Warnings go to standard error first; the create line is printed before it
runs and written to the audit log after.
.El
.Pp
.Ar spec
is the vdev grammar of
.Xr zpool-create 8
with disk names instead of paths: a kind word
.Pq Cm mirror , raidz1 , raidz2 , raidz3 , draid2 , ...
starts a vdev, a role word
.Pq Cm log , cache , spare , special , dedup
starts a section, and every other word is a disk from the shelf
.Pq Ql sda , Ql /dev/sda No or its by-id path .
A bare
.Cm draid2
gets an automatic group layout; the explicit form
.Ql draid2:4d:12c:1s
is accepted as is.
.Pp
Example \(em twelve 20 TB disks, two NVMe, two SSD:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zxplore --builder suggest tank sdc sdd sde sdf sdg sdh sdi sdj sdk sdl sdm sdn
RAIDZ2         RECOMMENDED     usable \(ap 200 TB   raw 240 TB   survives any 2 disk(s)
  two parity disks per vdev \(em survives a failure during a resilver; the default for large disks
  zpool create -o ashift=12 -O compression=zstd tank raidz2 /dev/disk/by-id/wwn-... log mirror /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-... cache /dev/disk/by-id/ata-...
.Ed
.Sh REMOTE OPERATION
A saved server is a name, an
.Ar user@host Ns Op : Ns Ar port ,
an optional dataset path that scopes what the pane shows, an optional jump
host, and a private key.
Everything runs over stock
.Xr ssh 1
\(em the remote end needs
.Em nothing
but OpenZFS and an SSH daemon.
No agent, no daemon, no zxplore install on the far side.
.Pp
When connecting,
.Nm
invokes ssh with
.Fl o Cm BatchMode=yes ,
.Fl o Cm ConnectTimeout=8 ,
.Fl o Cm IdentitiesOnly=yes
(always \(em so a loaded ssh-agent cannot exhaust the server's
.Cm MaxAuthTries
budget with unrelated keys), and, when a key is configured,
.Fl i Ar keyfile .
Because it is plain ssh, everything in your
.Pa ~/.ssh/config
still applies \(em including
.Cm Host
aliases, which are accepted in the host field (see
.Sx SSH CONFIGURATION ) .
.Pp
Replication streams
.Ql zfs send
into
.Ql zfs recv
for any local/remote combination \(em including remote\(->remote \(em into a
target received readonly and non-automounting so it can never drift out of
the incremental chain.
The pipeline picks the cheapest correct stream automatically:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
a RESUME
.Pq Ql send -t <token>
when the target holds a partial receive from an interrupted run \(em
receives run with
.Fl s ,
so interruptions leave a
.Ql receive_resume_token
instead of wasted work;
.It
an INCREMENTAL when the ends share a snapshot \(em or, if every common
snapshot was pruned on the source, a matching BOOKMARK
.Pq Ql send -i ds#bm ;
.It
a FULL send otherwise.
.El
.Pp
An encrypted source is sent RAW
.Pq Fl w :
blocks travel still-encrypted and the key never loads on the target \(em
the offsite box cannot read the data it stores.
Bookmark a snapshot from the snapshot menu to keep an incremental base that
costs no space, then prune the snapshot itself.
.Sh KEY SETUP
The intended flow is key-first: a password crosses the wire exactly once,
to install the key, and is never stored.
.Pp
The one-step path is
.Ic Set up & save
in the server editor: it generates a key when the server has none, skips
authorization when the key already works, otherwise installs it with the
one-time password, verifies ZFS is reachable, and saves the server.
.Pp
The same steps individually, when you want control:
.Bl -enum -offset indent -compact
.It
.Ic Servers\(->New :
name it, set host and user.
.It
.Ic Generate key
creates a fresh ed25519 keypair under
.Pa ~/.config/zxplore/keys/<name>
(mode 0600); or
.Ic Paste key
/
.Ic Use key file
to bring your own.
.It
.Ic Authorize on server
asks for the account password once and appends the public key to the
server's
.Pa ~/.ssh/authorized_keys .
.It
.Ic Test connection
verifies both the login and that ZFS is visible.
.El
.Pp
The same thing by hand, if you prefer your own tooling:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/zfs_fleet -C zfs-fleet
$ ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/zfs_fleet.pub zexp@box1.example.com
.Ed
.Pp
then point the server's key field at
.Pa ~/.ssh/zfs_fleet
with
.Ic Use key file .
One key can serve the whole fleet \(em authorize it everywhere and reuse
the path in every saved server.
.Sh SSH CONFIGURATION
Because every remote command is plain
.Xr ssh 1 ,
a few lines of
.Pa ~/.ssh/config
turn
.Nm
into a fleet tool.
A worked example for a fleet with a bastion:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# every ZFS box, one identity, multiplexed connections
Host zfs-*
    User zexp
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/zfs_fleet
    IdentitiesOnly yes
    ControlMaster auto
    ControlPath ~/.ssh/cm-%r@%h-%p
    ControlPersist 5m
    ServerAliveInterval 15

Host zfs-nyc1
    HostName 203.0.113.10
Host zfs-nyc2
    HostName 203.0.113.11

# the lab racks are only reachable through the bastion
Host zfs-lab*
    ProxyJump ops@bastion.example.com
.Ed
.Pp
With that in place, a saved server's host field is just
.Ql zfs-nyc1
\(em no user, port, or key needed in
.Nm
at all.
.Pp
.Cm ControlMaster Ns / Ns Cm ControlPersist
is the single most useful line for
.Nm :
each browse, dossier, and snapshot listing is its own ssh command, and
connection multiplexing turns every one after the first into a
millisecond-class round trip instead of a fresh TCP+auth handshake.
Remote browsing feels local.
.Pp
Jump hosts can also be set per saved server in the app (the
.Ic Jump host
field, comma-separated for multi-hop), which
.Nm
passes as
.Fl o Cm ProxyJump .
Command-line options passed by
.Nm
(BatchMode, ConnectTimeout, the identity) take precedence over the config
file, per
.Xr ssh_config 5 .
.Sh MANAGING A FLEET
The agentless recipe for N machines, once per machine, as root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# useradd -m zexp                      (FreeBSD: pw useradd zexp -m)
# install -d -m 700 -o zexp `eval echo ~zexp`/.ssh
# echo 'ssh-ed25519 AAAA... zfs-fleet' \e
    > `eval echo ~zexp`/.ssh/authorized_keys
# chown zexp `eval echo ~zexp`/.ssh/authorized_keys
# chmod 600 `eval echo ~zexp`/.ssh/authorized_keys
# zfs allow zexp \e
    send,snapshot,hold,diff,mount tank
.Ed
.Pp
That last line is the point: with
.Xr zfs-allow 8
delegation,
.Nm
drives the box read/replicate-wise with no root login and no sudo on the
remote at all.
Add
.Ql create,receive
on a backup target so it can accept streams:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs create tank/backups
# zfs allow zexp create,receive,mount tank/backups
.Ed
.Pp
Scope a saved server's
.Ic Path
field to
.Ql tank/backups
and the transfer pane shows exactly that subtree \(em which is also all a
delegated user can list.
.Pp
Headless boxes get the static binary; it has no dependencies:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ make zxplore-tui
$ for h in zfs-nyc1 zfs-nyc2 zfs-lab1; do
>   scp zxplore-tui $h:/usr/local/bin/zxplore
> done
.Ed
.Pp
Every mutating command
.Nm
runs \(em on any host \(em lands in the audit log with its timestamp,
target host, and exact argv, so the fleet's change history is one
.Xr grep 1
away.
.Sh PRIVILEGES
.Nm
runs unprivileged and elevates UNPRIVILEGED-FIRST: every command is tried as
the operator, and only a permission failure retries under
.Xr pkexec 1
\(em so root and
.Xr zfs-allow 8 Ns -delegated
users never see a prompt at all.
Remotely, commands always run as the connected (ideally delegated) user.
The installed polkit policy
.Pq Pa org.zxplore.policy
lets one admin authentication cover a few minutes of commands instead of
prompting per action.
.Pp
Both surfaces are READ-ONLY BY DEFAULT: the TUI unlocks with
.Ic :rw ,
the GUI with the toolbar lock button (red while unlocked).
Rollbacks, destroys, and restore-over-live always demand retyping the
target's name, in both.
Encryption passphrases travel on standard input, never on a command line.
On the remote side, grant a delegated user the needed permissions with
.Xr zfs-allow 8
rather than connecting as root; the
.Ql diff
permission additionally enables unprivileged
.Ql zfs diff .
.Pp
Useful delegation sets, from least to most trusted:
.Bl -tag -width "replication target" -offset indent -compact
.It read/observe
.Ql send,hold,diff,mount
.It operator
.Ql send,snapshot,hold,diff,mount,clone,promote
.It replication target
.Ql create,receive,mount
.El
.Sh KLDLOAD INTEGRATION
On a host running the kldload substrate,
.Nm
detects the platform (marker files plus which k-commands resolve in
.Ev PATH )
and lights up its extras: a green
.Dq kldload \(em N extra tools
chip in the header and the
.Ic Boot Envs
manager in the toolbar.
On any other ZFS host none of this appears \(em
.Nm
is fully generic and depends on nothing from kldload.
.Pp
The ZFS-related k-commands it detects:
.Bl -tag -width "krecovery" -offset indent -compact
.It Ic kbe
boot environments \(em create, list, activate, roll back, delete.
.It Ic ksnap
snapshot helper.
.It Ic kst
snapshot status.
.It Ic kclone
clone a dataset or snapshot.
.It Ic krecovery
recovery flow \(em import and roll back a boot environment.
.It Ic kexport
export a dataset.
.It Ic kimage
image a dataset.
.It Ic kinspect
inspect a dataset.
.It Ic kdf
ZFS-aware disk free.
.It Ic kdir
directory helper.
.El
.Pp
Boot environments in
.Nm
are driven by plain
.Xr zfs 8
against the boot dataset DERIVED from the pool's
.Ql bootfs
property \(em correct regardless of what the environment is named.
.Sh OBSERVE
The Observe tab (F5) is the debugger: one second of a pool, read for you.
A sample is two reads of the kernel's ZFS kstats
.Pq Pa /proc/spl/kstat/zfs , No or Xr sysctl 8 Cm kstat.zfs.misc No on Fx
around one
.Ql zpool iostat -l
interval, so every counter carries a per-second rate, plus
.Ql zpool status -s ,
.Ql zpool get ,
the memory and module tunables the verdicts compare against, and the last
events.
The verdicts are sentences, each naming the number it read and the property
or command that changes it, ordered red, gold, green, info:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
pool health; slow I/Os per device
.Pq the SLOW column ;
a device answering many times slower than its siblings; pool read and write
latency and which wait it is
.Pq disk, sync queue, async queue ;
.It
the write throttle \(em
.Cm dmu_tx_dirty_delay
and
.Cm dmu_tx_dirty_throttle
against
.Cm zfs_dirty_data_max
\(em and txg sync time against
.Cm zfs_txg_timeout ;
.It
ARC demand hit rate, whether the ARC is pinned at a cap far below the host's
memory, memory-pressure throttling;
.It
sync writes landing on the pool when there is no log vdev \(em with the
datasets doing it, from the per-dataset objset kstats \(em and ZIL commit
stalls;
.It
capacity, fragmentation, dedup enabled with a 1.00 ratio, prefetch that is
not helping, the busiest datasets, error reports among recent events.
.El
.Pp
The tab adds a Live toggle (a fresh sample every second), the per-vdev
latency table, the busiest datasets, the events, and a kstat browser that
shows any group with per-second rates.
Nothing here needs root; what does
.Pq Ql zpool events No on some hosts
is reported as a warning in the sample, never silently dropped.
.Pp
From a terminal:
.Bl -tag -width "--observe POOL kstat GROUP"
.It Nm Fl -observe Op Ar pool
One report: gauges, verdicts, vdevs, datasets, events.
.It Nm Fl -observe Oo Ar pool Oc Cm watch
The same, redrawn every second until interrupted.
.It Nm Fl -observe Oo Ar pool Oc Cm json
The sample and its verdicts as JSON, for a script or a ticket.
.It Nm Fl -observe Oo Ar pool Oc Cm kstat Ar group
One kstat group
.Pq Cm arcstats , zil , dmu_tx , zfetchstats , dbufstats , Ar pool Ns /txgs
with its counters and their per-second rates.
.It Nm Fl -observe Oo Ar pool Oc Cm events
.Ql zpool events -v .
.El
.Pp
What Observe does not do yet is name the process: that is eBPF on Linux and
.Xr dtrace 1
on
.Fx ,
the next layer.
The dataset-level answer is usually the one wanted.
.Sh CONTAINERS
On a host whose container engine uses the ZFS storage driver, every image
layer is a real dataset and the store also holds the engine's database, the
layer graph and the volumes.
The whole container estate is therefore one snapshottable, replicable unit,
and this is the terminal path to it.
.Pp
Both engines are handled by the same code path:
.Xr docker 1
where it is installed, otherwise
.Xr podman 1 .
Neither is required \(em without one, the tab and these commands are absent
rather than empty.
.Bl -tag -width "--containers snapshot NAME"
.It Nm Fl -containers
What is running, what images are local, and \(em the point \(em which
storage driver is in use and whether layers are datasets or ordinary files.
.It Nm Fl -containers Cm snapshots
List the estate's snapshots.
.It Nm Fl -containers Cm snapshot Ar name
Capture every image layer, the engine's database and the volumes in one
recursive snapshot.
.It Nm Fl -containers Cm rollback Ar name
Return all of it to that point.
The engine must be stopped first: rolling storage out from under an open
store corrupts it, and this refuses while the daemon is answering.
.It Nm Fl -containers Cm replicate
Print the
.Xr zfs 8
send command that moves the estate to another host, incremental form
included.
It prints rather than runs \(em the far side is a host, a key and a receiving
dataset this command cannot know.
.El
.Pp
On an encrypted pool the printed command uses a raw stream
.Pq Fl w ,
because
.Ql zfs send -R
refuses on an encrypted dataset.
The blocks stay encrypted in transit; the receiving host needs the key to
mount them, not to store them.
.Pp
.Sy Note :
rollback and snapshot need privilege and will elevate.
Listing works unprivileged wherever the engine itself does.
.Sh THE TERMINAL UI
.Nm Fl -tui
(and the static
.Nm zxplore-tui
binary) is a full console, not a fallback.
Four views: the browser with live dossier (F1), the dual-pane transfer
commander (F2), the snapshot file explorer (F3 or
.Ic x
on a dataset), and the pools view (F4) with the full drill-down dossier.
The Builder has no terminal view yet; its engine is
.Fl -builder .
.Ic \&?
shows every key inside the app; this is the same list.
.Pp
Browser (F1):
.Bl -tag -width "ctrl+d / ctrl+u" -offset indent -compact
.It Ic Up , Ic Down , Ic j , Ic k
move;
.Ic g Ns / Ns Ic G
first / last;
.Ic ctrl+d Ns / Ns Ic ctrl+u
half page.
.It Ic /
filter the dataset list.
.It Ic Enter
snapshot menu on the dataset: explore, diff against live or any snapshot,
clone, bookmark, rollback, destroy.
.It Ic a
dataset menu: create child or zvol, rename, mount, encryption
(load, unload, change key, create encrypted), destroy.
.It Ic Tab
edit properties in the right pane: arrows walk the settable properties,
Enter changes one \(em enum and on/off properties get the same option lists
the GUI offers, free-form ones a text input;
.Ic Tab
or Escape returns.
.It Ic x
the file explorer on the dataset.
.It Ic s
snapshot now.
.It Ic b
bookmark the current location (see
.Ic c ) .
.It Ic c
connect: pick a saved location \(em a host, or a host and dataset \(em and
re-home the browser there.
Locations are the ones bookmarked with
.Ic b ,
most recent first (see
.Sx FILES ) .
.It Ic B
boot environments: create, roll back, delete (also
.Ic :be ) .
.It Ic r
reload.
.It Ic q
quit.
.El
.Pp
Explorer (F3):
.Bl -tag -width "ctrl+d / ctrl+u" -offset indent -compact
.It Ic Enter , Ic l
enter a directory or pick a file;
.Ic h
or Backspace goes up.
.It Ic Tab
hop between the files pane and the versions pane
(every snapshot holding the path).
.It Ic \&[ , Ic \&]
browse the live tree or inside a snapshot.
.It Ic r
restore as a copy;
.Ic R
restore OVER live (needs
.Ic :rw
and a typed confirmation).
.It Ic d
diff the snapshot against live.
.El
.Pp
Pools (F4):
.Bl -tag -width "ctrl+d / ctrl+u" -offset indent -compact
.It Ic Enter , Ic d
drill-down dossier: vitals, zfs-vs-df space, vdevs with error counters,
iostat, ARC, events.
.It Ic s Ic S
start / stop a scrub.
.It Ic t
trim.
.It Ic c
clear device errors.
.It Ic i
scan for importable pools (import with
.Ic :importpool ) .
.El
.Pp
The command bar
.Pq Ic \&:
takes
.Ic :browse , Ic :transfer , Ic :pools , Ic :explore Op Ar dataset ,
.Ic :connect Ar user@host:pool , Ic :importpool Ar name , Ic :be ,
.Ic :ro
/
.Ic :rw ,
.Ic :help
and
.Ic :q .
The verbs the menus offer are commands too \(em
.Ic :snaps , Ic :clone , Ic :rollback , Ic :hold , Ic :release , Ic :restore ,
.Ic :scrub , Ic :trim , Ic :loadkey , Ic :unloadkey , Ic :changekey ,
.Ic :setprop , Ic :bookmark , Ic :refresh
\(em and
.Ic :help
lists them with their arguments.
.Pp
The TUI is READ-ONLY BY DEFAULT: every mutation \(em snapshot, restore,
rollback, replicate, pool maintenance \(em requires
.Ic :rw
first (the header badge flips from RO to a red RW;
.Ic :ro
relocks), and rollback, destroy, and restore-over-live additionally demand
retyping the target's name.
Browsing, diffing, and drill-downs never need unlocking.
.Sh KEY BINDINGS (GUI)
.Bl -tag -width "PgUp/PgDn, Home/End" -compact
.It F1 / F2 / F3 / F4 / F5 / F6
switch Browser / Builder / Transfer / Explorer / Observe / Containers.
Builder (F2) and Observe (F5) arrived in 1.3.0; Transfer, Explorer and
Containers are F3, F4 and F6 since.
.It Tab
hop between panes (dataset list/snapshots; transfer left/right; explorer
files/versions).
.It Arrows, PgUp/PgDn, Home/End
move; the detail pane follows.
.It Ctrl+F or /
find (live filter).
.It Enter
act on the highlighted row: dataset lifecycle menu, snapshot action menu,
enter directory, restore menu \(em all arrow-navigable.
.It \&?
open this manual inside the app (it ships embedded in the binary).
.It Right-click
dataset lifecycle menu (same as Enter).
.It Escape
dismiss the topmost dialog or window.
.It Alt+Q
quit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "ZXPLORE_BUILDER_ALL_DEVICES" -compact
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
where
.Pa zxplore/servers.json , zxplore/keys/
and
.Pa zxplore/favorites.json
live; defaults to
.Pa ~/.config .
.It Ev HOME
the audit log's home
.Pq Pa ~/.local/state/zxplore/audit.log
and the default for
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.It Ev USER
the user offered when a saved server has none.
.It Ev PATH
searched for the
.Xr zfs 8
and
.Xr zpool 8
binaries, the container engine, and the kldload k-commands whose presence
lights up the kldload extras.
.It Ev ZXPLORE_BUILDER_ALL_DEVICES
when set to 1, the Builder's shelf includes pseudo block devices \(em zvols,
loop devices, ramdisks, device-mapper and md \(em that are normally hidden
because nothing real is ever built on them.
For labs and tests.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.local/state/zxplore/audit.log
one line per executed mutating command: RFC 3339 timestamp, target host,
exact argv.
Passphrases never appear (they are not argv).
.It Pa ~/.config/zxplore/servers.json
saved servers.
Contains no passwords \(em key paths only.
.It Pa ~/.config/zxplore/keys/
private keys generated or pasted in the app, mode 0600.
.It Pa ~/.config/zxplore/favorites.json
saved locations for the terminal UI's
.Ic c
connect menu: a name, an optional
.Ar user@host
and a dataset path, most recent first.
.It Pa MOUNTPOINT/.zfs/snapshot
the per-dataset snapshot directory the explorer reads.
Works whether the
.Ql snapdir
property is
.Ql hidden
or
.Ql visible .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run the console, headless:
.Pp
.Dl $ zxplore --tui
.Pp
Build the static terminal binary and drop it on a storage box:
.Pp
.Dl $ make zxplore-tui && scp zxplore-tui box:/usr/local/bin/zxplore
.Pp
Create and authorize a fleet key by hand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/zfs_fleet -C zfs-fleet
$ ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/zfs_fleet.pub zexp@zfs-nyc1
.Ed
.Pp
Delegate enough for a user to browse, snapshot, and diff, unprivileged:
.Pp
.Dl # zfs allow carol send,snapshot,hold,diff,mount tank/data
.Pp
Prepare a backup box to receive replication from zxplore:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs create tank/backups
# zfs allow zexp create,receive,mount tank/backups
.Ed
.Pp
What the explorer's
.Dq restore from snapshot
runs under the hood \(em the same thing you could type:
.Pp
.Dl # cp -a /tank/data/.zfs/snapshot/daily-2026-07-20/etc/pf.conf /tank/data/etc/pf.conf.from-daily-2026-07-20
.Pp
Review everything
.Nm
has ever changed, newest last:
.Pp
.Dl $ tail ~/.local/state/zxplore/audit.log
.Pp
Snapshot the container estate before an upgrade, then put it back if the
upgrade breaks:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zxplore --containers snapshot before-upgrade
# ... the upgrade eats the database ...
systemctl stop docker
zxplore --containers rollback before-upgrade
systemctl start docker
.Ed
.Pp
Move an entire container estate \(em images, containers and volumes \(em to
another machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zxplore --containers snapshot move
zxplore --containers replicate   # prints the send command
.Ed
.Pp
Print the version:
.Pp
.Dl $ zxplore --version
.Pp
The disk shelf of the box you are on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zxplore --builder disks
nvme0n1      nvme       2.0 TB  Samsung SSD 980 PRO 2TB      in use: mounted at /boot/efi, zfs member of rpool
sda          hdd         20 TB  WDC WD201KFGX                free
sdb          hdd         20 TB  WDC WD201KFGX                free
.Ed
.Pp
What eight new disks could be, with the create line for each answer:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zxplore --builder suggest tank sda sdb sdc sdd sde sdf sdg sdh
RAIDZ2         RECOMMENDED     usable \(ap 120 TB   raw 160 TB   survives any 2 disk(s)
  two parity disks per vdev \(em survives a failure during a resilver; the default for large disks
  zpool create -o ashift=12 -O compression=zstd tank raidz2 /dev/disk/by-id/wwn-... ...
dRAID2         FAST RESILVER   usable \(ap 93 TB    raw 160 TB   survives any 2 disk(s)
  ...
.Ed
.Pp
Ask zpool what it would build, then build it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zxplore --builder dry-run tank raidz2 sda sdb sdc sdd sde sdf log mirror nvme1n1 nvme2n1 spare sdg
would create 'tank' with the following layout:

	tank
	  raidz2
	    wwn-0x5000cca2a1
	    ...
$ zxplore --builder create tank raidz2 sda sdb sdc sdd sde sdf log mirror nvme1n1 nvme2n1 spare sdg
zpool create -o ashift=12 -O compression=zstd tank raidz2 /dev/disk/by-id/wwn-... log mirror ... spare ...
pool tank created
.Ed
.Pp
The vdev tree of a pool that exists, error counters included:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zxplore --builder topology tank
tank                                     ONLINE  r/w/c 0/0/0
  raidz2-0                                 ONLINE  r/w/c 0/0/0
    /dev/disk/by-id/wwn-0x5000cca2a1         ONLINE  r/w/c 0/0/0
  spares
    /dev/disk/by-id/wwn-0x5000cca2a7         AVAIL
.Ed
.Pp
A lab: build a pool on four loop files, without touching a real disk:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# for i in 0 1 2 3; do truncate -s 1G /var/tmp/d$i.img; losetup -f /var/tmp/d$i.img; done
# ZXPLORE_BUILDER_ALL_DEVICES=1 zxplore --builder create lab raidz1 loop0 loop1 loop2 spare loop3
.Ed
.Pp
What is running, and whether its layers are datasets:
.Pp
.Dl $ zxplore --containers list
.Pp
Why is the pool slow right now:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zxplore --observe tank
tank \(em 11:37:20 (1.0 s sample)
  ARC 100% hit \(pc 17 GB of 17 GB
  ops 0 r / 55 w \(pc 0 B/s r / 229 kB/s w
  ...
VERDICTS
  [GOLD] sync writes are landing on the main pool
        21.5 kB/s of ZIL blocks/s, 56 commits/s, no log vdev; from: tank/db 21.5 kB/s
        fix: a mirrored SLOG (Builder, role log) absorbs them; or sync=disabled ...
.Ed
.Pp
Watch it while the load runs, and keep the numbers for the ticket:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zxplore --observe tank watch
$ zxplore --observe tank json > tank-slow.json
.Ed
.Pp
The ARC counters with rates, and a pool's last transaction groups:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ zxplore --observe kstat arcstats
$ zxplore --observe tank kstat tank/txgs
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Every failure names the command that failed and repeats what
.Xr zfs 8
or
.Xr zpool 8
said, verbatim; nothing is paraphrased.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dq zxplore: no such disk on the shelf: Ar name
A
.Fl -builder
spec named a disk that
.Fl -builder Cm disks
does not list.
Pseudo devices need
.Ev ZXPLORE_BUILDER_ALL_DEVICES .
.It Dq zxplore: Ar disk No is in use ( Ar reason )
The design touches a disk that carries a filesystem, a partition or a pool
member.
The Builder never offers it; wipe it first if you mean it.
.It Dq lsblk: ... ( Fx No hosts have no lsblk ... )
The Builder shelf is Linux-only for now.
.It Dq Running in chroot, modules won't be signed
Not from
.Nm
\(em quoted here because operators grep for it: it is what dkms says on a
host whose framework configuration lacks
.Ql try_sign_modules=true .
.It Dq zpool create -n ... : Ar text
The dry run is zpool refusing the design; its text is the reason
.Pq a busy device, a label it will not overwrite, an ashift it will not honour .
.It Dq permission denied
locally, the command is retried under
.Xr pkexec 1 ;
remotely it means the delegated user lacks a
.Xr zfs-allow 8
permission, which the message names.
.El
.Pp
In the GUI the same text appears in a dialog; in both surfaces every
executed mutation is in the audit log (see
.Sx FILES )
whether it succeeded or not.
